For each pair: get it, research it if it is unclear, then decide. Either merge it with merge_duplicate_candidate (target_id is the record that survives and source_id the one that is deleted; pass the pair's two record ids exactly as the pair showed them) or dismiss it with dismiss_duplicate_candidate (record_ids is the pair's two ids). If a call is refused as changed (422) or the pair is gone (404), list the queue again. Give every merge and dismissal a note. You are authorised to merge and dismiss without asking me. If you genuinely cannot tell, skip it and say why.

House rules, from past decisions:

- The English edition is the canonical book and should survive a merge.
- "Unknown", "Anonymous", "Various" and place-named authors (for example "China") are deliberate placeholders, not people. Never merge a person into them or treat them as wrong.
- A collection, omnibus or poetry umbrella book is not a duplicate of the works inside it.
- Translations of the same work are one book. A translator credited as author is a data problem, not a reason to keep two books.
- Prefer the survivor with the better rank, more lists and better identifiers, unless the rules above say otherwise.

Notes: one or two sentences per pair, with the evidence, for example a URL or an identifier.

When you finish, report what you merged, dismissed and skipped, each with the pair id and one line of reasoning. Include any merge warnings verbatim.
